[0.935,"o","$ "]
[0.936,"o","ov stack deploy openvidu-e2e-oci-ce-s125537 --cloud oracle --oci-region \"$OCI_REGION\" --oci-compartment \"$OCI_COMPARTMENT_OCID\" --edition community --certificate-type selfsigned --mode assisted\r\n"]
[2.052,"o","warning: the stack publishes into the vault *** (--oci-vault), which is not its own. Its secrets have fixed names (OPENVIDU_URL, the credentials, the license, the stack's access), so two stacks in one vault mix them; every deploy adds versions to the same secrets until Oracle refuses more (LimitExceeded) and the stack never publishes; and a destroy leaves this stack's credentials in it. Give every stack a vault of its own: leave --oci-vault out and the stack creates one\r\n"]
[7.84,"o","note: recorded this stack's infrastructure in openvidu-stack.yml, so the command that continues this deploy needs no flags\r\n"]
[7.841,"o","── PLAN · nothing below has run ───────────────────────────────────────\r\nRun these commands yourself in your Oracle Cloud tenancy (region eu-madrid-1, compartment ***); ov runs none of them.\r\nOnce they have finished, run this same command again: ov finds the machines and ends the deploy.\r\n\r\n  # Terraform authenticates with your OCI API key (~/.oci/config); so does the state\r\n  # bucket's backend, which needs Terraform 1.12.0 or later.\r\n  # Read before anything is created (reads only): no dynamic group or policy of the\r\n  # tenancy has this stack's names (openvidu-e2e-oci-ce-s125537-af17f42d-instances-dg,\r\n  # …); the region eu-madrid-1 has 1 availability domain(s); pJhl:EU-MADRID-1-AD-1\r\n  # offers VM.Standard.E4.Flex; standard-e4-core-count: 4 OCPUs needed in\r\n  # pJhl:EU-MADRID-1-AD-1, 100 free; standard-e4-memory-count: 8 GB of memory needed in\r\n  # pJhl:EU-MADRID-1-AD-1, 1666 free; the user has 0 of the 2 Customer Secret Keys Oracle\r\n  # allows.\r\n  # Warning: the stack publishes into the vault\r\n  # ***\r\n  # (--oci-vault), which is not its own. Its secrets have fixed names (OPENVIDU_URL, the\r\n  # credentials, the license, the stack's access), so two stacks in one vault mix them;\r\n  # every deploy adds versions to the same secrets until Oracle refuses more\r\n  # (LimitExceeded) and the stack never publishes; and a destroy leaves this stack's\r\n  # credentials in it. Give every stack a vault of its own: leave --oci-vault out and the\r\n  # stack creates one\r\n  # The template, rendered from the one embedded in this CLI for the single node topology,\r\n  # and its variables, written out for you to read (the variables file is 0600: it\r\n  # can carry secrets, such as Meet's seed credentials or a private key):\r\n  #   .openvidu/oracle/tf-oracle-openvidu-singlenode.tf\r\n  #   .openvidu/oracle/openvidu.auto.tfvars.json\r\n  terraform -chdir=.openvidu/oracle init -input=false -no-color -force-copy\r\n  # If this stack already exists, read first what the apply would change: a new script an\r\n  # instance boots with (the domain, the certificate, the Meet credentials or another\r\n  # OpenVidu CLI) REPLACES that instance, which starts again from scratch with a new\r\n  # public address, and Terraform's plan says \"must be replaced\"; a lower number of media\r\n  # nodes drains the ones that go first:\r\n  terraform -chdir=.openvidu/oracle plan\r\n  # Create the machine. deployedBy=instance: it installs OpenVidu on itself with the\r\n  # OpenVidu CLI. `apply` returns when Terraform is done (about 3 minutes, then OpenVidu installs); OpenVidu comes up after.\r\n  terraform -chdir=.openvidu/oracle apply -auto-approve -input=false -no-color\r\n\r\n  # Read back where the stack is (sensitive outputs, such as a generated SSH key, show as\r\n  # <sensitive>: `terraform output -raw <name>` prints one).\r\n  terraform -chdir=.openvidu/oracle output\r\n  # What the stack published: URL and credentials, as secrets in the stack's vault, base64 (OPENVIDU_URL appears once\r\n  # OpenVidu serves: until then this read fails, and ov waits for it).\r\n  VAULT=\"$(terraform -chdir=.openvidu/oracle output -raw vault_id)\"\r\n  oci secrets secret-bundle get-secret-bundle-by-name --secret-name OPENVIDU_URL --vault-id $VAULT --query 'data.\"secret-bundle-content\".content' --raw-output --region eu-madrid-1 | base64 -d\r\n\r\nNothing above has been run. Once these commands have finished, run this same command again:\r\n\r\n  ov stack deploy openvidu-e2e-oci-ce-s125537 --cloud oracle --mode assisted\r\n\r\nIt finds the machines, waits for OpenVidu to come up and registers this directory as the\r\nstack. It creates nothing in your account.\r\n── end of plan ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\r\n\r\n"]
[8.02,"m","The runbook's commands, exactly as ov printed them 1/6"]
[8.02,"o","\u001b[2m# Terraform authenticates with your OCI API key (~/.oci/config); so does the state\r\n# bucket's backend, which needs Terraform 1.12.0 or later.\r\n# Read before anything is created (reads only): no dynamic group or policy of the\r\n# tenancy has this stack's names (openvidu-e2e-oci-ce-s125537-af17f42d-instances-dg,\r\n# …); the region eu-madrid-1 has 1 availability domain(s); pJhl:EU-MADRID-1-AD-1\r\n# offers VM.Standard.E4.Flex; standard-e4-core-count: 4 OCPUs needed in\r\n# pJhl:EU-MADRID-1-AD-1, 100 free; standard-e4-memory-count: 8 GB of memory needed in\r\n# pJhl:EU-MADRID-1-AD-1, 1666 free; the user has 0 of the 2 Customer Secret Keys Oracle\r\n# allows.\r\n# Warning: the stack publishes into the vault\r\n# ***\r\n# (--oci-vault), which is not its own. Its secrets have fixed names (OPENVIDU_URL, the\r\n# credentials, the license, the stack's access), so two stacks in one vault mix them;\r\n# every deploy adds versions to the same secrets until Oracle refuses more\r\n# (LimitExceeded) and the stack never publishes; and a destroy leaves this stack's\r\n# credentials in it. Give every stack a vault of its own: leave --oci-vault out and the\r\n# stack creates one\r\n# The template, rendered from the one embedded in this CLI for the single node topology,\r\n# and its variables, written out for you to read (the variables file is 0600: it\r\n# can carry secrets, such as Meet's seed credentials or a private key):\r\n#   .openvidu/oracle/tf-oracle-openvidu-singlenode.tf\r\n#   .openvidu/oracle/openvidu.auto.tfvars.json\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[8.021,"o","$ "]
[8.021,"o","terraform -chdir=.openvidu/oracle init -input=false -no-color -force-copy\r\n"]
